WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.699 --> 00:00:04.616
(triumphant music)

00:00:11.934 --> 00:00:14.601
(flag flapping)

00:00:16.227 --> 00:00:19.810
(The Star Spangled Banner)

00:01:02.930 --> 00:01:07.347
(The Star Spangled Banner continues)

00:01:36.148 --> 00:01:38.815
(flag flapping)

00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:44.170
- Yale has a proud
tradition of involvement in

00:01:44.170 --> 00:01:47.510
and ties to military service.

00:01:47.510 --> 00:01:50.870
Many of us have walked by
the statue of Nathan Hale,

00:01:50.870 --> 00:01:53.450
who sacrificed his life for his country

00:01:53.450 --> 00:01:54.953
in the Revolutionary War.

00:01:56.071 --> 00:01:58.580
And we have read the
cenotaph on the quadrangle

00:01:58.580 --> 00:02:00.890
that honors the hundreds of Yale students

00:02:00.890 --> 00:02:03.530
and alumni who died in World War I.

00:02:04.730 --> 00:02:06.920
Through our shared history,

00:02:06.920 --> 00:02:09.670
the Yale community is
connected to thousands

00:02:09.670 --> 00:02:11.700
of Yalies who served in the First

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:15.563
and Second World Wars, Korea, and Vietnam.

00:02:16.970 --> 00:02:20.480
This connection extends to
the faculty, students, staff,

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:22.423
and alumni who serve today.

00:02:23.490 --> 00:02:25.790
The spirit of service and sacrifice

00:02:25.790 --> 00:02:28.210
that link our military personnel

00:02:28.210 --> 00:02:30.323
is a hallmark of Yale's mission.

00:02:31.510 --> 00:02:34.640
Our university is characterized
by a deep commitment

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:37.450
to the common good and to the security

00:02:37.450 --> 00:02:39.763
and prosperity of generations to come.

00:02:40.730 --> 00:02:42.200
In these turbulent times,

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:46.410
I am more grateful than
ever for all those who work

00:02:46.410 --> 00:02:49.200
to strengthen the fabric
of our university,

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:52.683
of our country, and of
communities around the globe.

00:02:53.650 --> 00:02:56.100
I appreciate deeply our faculty, staff,

00:02:56.100 --> 00:02:58.500
and alumni who are veterans.

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:01.170
Yale is committed to
supporting our veterans

00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:04.310
through programs like the
Yale Veterans Network,

00:03:04.310 --> 00:03:08.000
a campus group of students,
faculty, and staff.

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:11.943
And the Yale Veterans Association,
an alumni organization.

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:15.870
And Yale is proud of its
students who currently serve

00:03:15.870 --> 00:03:19.703
in the Reserve Officers
Training Corps on campus.

00:03:20.840 --> 00:03:25.700
The COVID-19 pandemic has
shown us how necessary it is

00:03:25.700 --> 00:03:29.450
that two great institutions
of American society,

00:03:29.450 --> 00:03:33.810
its universities and its
professional armed services

00:03:33.810 --> 00:03:36.380
are here to educate future leaders

00:03:36.380 --> 00:03:40.533
in how to handle the trials
and uncertainties in life.

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:43.200
As we mark Veteran's Day,

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:45.480
it is vitally important for us

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:48.193
to remember the human toll of war.

00:03:49.510 --> 00:03:51.260
Individuals have made

00:03:51.260 --> 00:03:53.870
and continue to make
extraordinary sacrifices

00:03:53.870 --> 00:03:55.383
in service to this country.

00:03:56.490 --> 00:03:59.813
Our veterans have my deepest
gratitude and respect.

00:04:00.690 --> 00:04:05.463
Today, and every day we
thank them for their service.

00:04:06.679 --> 00:04:09.846
(Armed Forces Medley)

00:04:52.182 --> 00:04:56.182
(Armed Forces Medley continues)

00:05:30.212 --> 00:05:34.212
(Armed Forces Medley continues)

00:05:58.841 --> 00:06:02.841
(Armed Forces Medley continues)

00:06:40.154 --> 00:06:44.154
(Armed Forces Medley continues)

00:07:30.661 --> 00:07:32.270
(patriotic music)

00:07:32.270 --> 00:07:34.510
- To begin I'd like to
thank President Salovey

00:07:34.510 --> 00:07:36.840
and Secretary Goff-Crews
for leading the university

00:07:36.840 --> 00:07:38.820
and honoring veterans each year.

00:07:38.820 --> 00:07:40.250
I also want to thank Dean Sodi,

00:07:40.250 --> 00:07:42.930
Patricia Wei and Jack
Beecher, who each play a role

00:07:42.930 --> 00:07:45.740
in ensuring the success
of student veterans.

00:07:45.740 --> 00:07:48.140
In 2011, I was looking for some excitement

00:07:48.140 --> 00:07:49.770
and a way to pay for college.

00:07:49.770 --> 00:07:51.330
Convinced I knew what I was doing,

00:07:51.330 --> 00:07:53.450
I enlisted in the Marine Corps.

00:07:53.450 --> 00:07:54.940
After working on unmanned aircraft

00:07:54.940 --> 00:07:56.780
and serving as a Marine security guard

00:07:56.780 --> 00:07:58.350
at Embassies and Consulates,

00:07:58.350 --> 00:08:01.050
I decided to earn a degree
at an officer's commission

00:08:01.050 --> 00:08:03.700
through the Marine Corps
Enlisted to Officer Program.

00:08:04.700 --> 00:08:07.010
In 2017, after being accepted

00:08:07.010 --> 00:08:09.560
to Yale's Eli Whitney students Program,

00:08:09.560 --> 00:08:11.460
I moved to Connecticut
to begin my assignment

00:08:11.460 --> 00:08:12.963
as a full-time undergraduate.

00:08:13.860 --> 00:08:16.750
After settling in, I couldn't
wait to meet my neighbors.

00:08:16.750 --> 00:08:18.620
I was especially curious about Maryann,

00:08:18.620 --> 00:08:20.800
who lives three doors down.

00:08:20.800 --> 00:08:22.600
Maryann is 92 years old,

00:08:22.600 --> 00:08:24.810
and she spends most of
her days pulling weeds

00:08:24.810 --> 00:08:27.260
and trimming flowers in the garden.

00:08:27.260 --> 00:08:30.600
Before COVID-19, she went to
the gym three times a week.

00:08:30.600 --> 00:08:32.390
When her gym decided to close in March,

00:08:32.390 --> 00:08:33.880
she was outraged and told me

00:08:33.880 --> 00:08:37.130
that she just didn't know
how she would stay in shape.

00:08:37.130 --> 00:08:39.560
Maryann and I don't seem
to have much in common.

00:08:39.560 --> 00:08:42.340
She was born in 1928 and was 90 years

00:08:42.340 --> 00:08:45.290
before all military jobs opened to women.

00:08:45.290 --> 00:08:47.680
She attended New Haven's
Hillhouse High School,

00:08:47.680 --> 00:08:49.580
where she learned how to cook and sew.

00:08:50.900 --> 00:08:52.410
Despite the many differences in decades

00:08:52.410 --> 00:08:55.550
between Maryann and me, we
do have one thing in common.

00:08:55.550 --> 00:08:57.223
Her husband, George was a Marine.

00:08:58.320 --> 00:09:01.720
Also a New Havener,
George was born in 1923

00:09:01.720 --> 00:09:04.730
and was Commercial High
School's star basketball player.

00:09:04.730 --> 00:09:06.560
When Maryann showed me his team picture,

00:09:06.560 --> 00:09:08.950
she wanted me to know that
despite being the shortest

00:09:08.950 --> 00:09:11.620
on the team, he was their best player.

00:09:11.620 --> 00:09:13.110
Like many in his generation,

00:09:13.110 --> 00:09:16.600
he enlisted soon after graduating in 1941.

00:09:16.600 --> 00:09:18.385
George would go on to fight two

00:09:18.385 --> 00:09:19.910
of the War's most historic battles.

00:09:19.910 --> 00:09:21.915
At Iwo Jima where Marines

00:09:21.915 --> 00:09:24.190
and a Navy corpsman raised
the flag on Mount Suribachi

00:09:24.190 --> 00:09:26.993
and on Okinawa, the bloodiest
battle of the Pacific War.

00:09:28.480 --> 00:09:30.700
Maryann has told me
that George never talked

00:09:30.700 --> 00:09:31.800
to her about the war,

00:09:31.800 --> 00:09:34.140
but that he was a Marine
through and through.

00:09:34.140 --> 00:09:35.970
That he loved his country, his family,

00:09:35.970 --> 00:09:38.140
and that he was proud to have served.

00:09:38.140 --> 00:09:40.340
He was outgoing and in Maryann’s words,

00:09:40.340 --> 00:09:42.090
he had a lot of parties.

00:09:42.090 --> 00:09:44.070
She told me that if he were alive today,

00:09:44.070 --> 00:09:46.000
he would have had some
great stories to tell me,

00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:47.930
but that he never exaggerated.

00:09:47.930 --> 00:09:49.760
To be fair, I don't think
there'd be much room

00:09:49.760 --> 00:09:50.793
for embellishment.

00:09:51.750 --> 00:09:53.090
After returning from the war,

00:09:53.090 --> 00:09:55.540
he met Maryann at Franklin
Street's Falcon Dance Hall,

00:09:55.540 --> 00:09:57.983
on Valentines Day 1946.

00:09:58.840 --> 00:10:01.470
They were married the next year,
settled into civilian life,

00:10:01.470 --> 00:10:04.450
and eventually moved to the
street we live on today.

00:10:04.450 --> 00:10:05.680
When George was in his 50s,

00:10:05.680 --> 00:10:07.810
he enrolled at the University of New Haven

00:10:07.810 --> 00:10:09.970
and earned a degree in business.

00:10:09.970 --> 00:10:12.110
Maryann was very proud when she told me

00:10:12.110 --> 00:10:13.990
that this degree usually took four years,

00:10:13.990 --> 00:10:15.930
but that George did it in two.

00:10:15.930 --> 00:10:17.100
I laughed when she said this,

00:10:17.100 --> 00:10:20.448
thinking about Zach Metcalf, Marine,

00:10:20.448 --> 00:10:22.240
and Timothy Dwight, class of 2018,

00:10:22.240 --> 00:10:23.603
who also did it in two.

00:10:25.230 --> 00:10:27.593
George and Maryann were
married for 70 years.

00:10:28.660 --> 00:10:30.090
What I've learned from Maryann,

00:10:30.090 --> 00:10:32.740
besides how great life can be at 92,

00:10:32.740 --> 00:10:34.280
is that the pride that comes with service

00:10:34.280 --> 00:10:35.740
is not only felt by us.

00:10:35.740 --> 00:10:37.780
It's also felt by our families.

00:10:37.780 --> 00:10:40.190
This is part of the reason we celebrate.

00:10:40.190 --> 00:10:42.320
Veterans Day is for those who have served.

00:10:42.320 --> 00:10:44.870
And it's for Maryann, for
people like my own parents.

00:10:44.870 --> 00:10:47.610
And for many of you listening
today who admire the service

00:10:47.610 --> 00:10:49.573
and sacrifice of someone close to you.

00:10:50.730 --> 00:10:52.080
I'm reminded of this each time

00:10:52.080 --> 00:10:54.610
Maryann asked me about my
experiences in the Marine Corps

00:10:54.610 --> 00:10:57.570
or invites me inside to
show me pictures of George.

00:10:57.570 --> 00:11:00.770
I'm also reminded of this
when the Naval ROTC midshipman

00:11:00.770 --> 00:11:04.040
and I gather on the steps
of Sterling Memorial Library

00:11:04.040 --> 00:11:06.110
and campus visitors stopped to tell us

00:11:06.110 --> 00:11:07.930
about their son or
daughter who was serving,

00:11:07.930 --> 00:11:11.360
or their own stories from
the Navy or the Marine Corps.

00:11:11.360 --> 00:11:13.760
I think this message is
especially fitting this year,

00:11:13.760 --> 00:11:15.200
when although we can't gather together

00:11:15.200 --> 00:11:17.690
to celebrate students, faculty, staff,

00:11:17.690 --> 00:11:20.500
and alumni, we're joined
virtually by more friends

00:11:20.500 --> 00:11:22.980
and family than probably ever before.

00:11:22.980 --> 00:11:25.810
So to all veterans past,
present and future,

00:11:25.810 --> 00:11:27.760
thank you for your service
and your sacrifice,

00:11:27.760 --> 00:11:28.963
and happy Veteran's Day.

00:11:30.715 --> 00:11:34.048
(trumpets playing Taps)

00:12:29.140 --> 00:12:31.320
- Our loving and merciful God

00:12:31.320 --> 00:12:34.650
of many names and all generations.

00:12:34.650 --> 00:12:38.220
We are thankful beyond measure
for the courage, commitment,

00:12:38.220 --> 00:12:40.710
and sacrifices of our veterans

00:12:40.710 --> 00:12:43.520
who have served bravely and selflessly

00:12:43.520 --> 00:12:48.283
in the United States Armed
Forces on land, sea, or sky.

00:12:49.180 --> 00:12:51.760
The legacy of their service to our country

00:12:51.760 --> 00:12:56.120
and to our world is one that
must never be forgotten.

00:12:56.120 --> 00:13:00.010
We remember with deep respect
and unending gratitude,

00:13:00.010 --> 00:13:03.410
those who have lost their
lives in this service.

00:13:03.410 --> 00:13:05.420
we are forever in their debt

00:13:05.420 --> 00:13:08.090
and we honor their families whose loss

00:13:08.090 --> 00:13:10.073
can never really be made whole.

00:13:10.960 --> 00:13:14.990
Gracious God, we also
ask for continued healing

00:13:14.990 --> 00:13:16.870
for all those who struggle

00:13:16.870 --> 00:13:20.623
with the visible and
invisible scars of war.

00:13:21.610 --> 00:13:25.080
We asked special regard
for those who have served,

00:13:25.080 --> 00:13:29.110
but who have not been
found and returned home.

00:13:29.110 --> 00:13:31.370
And for those who have returned,

00:13:31.370 --> 00:13:34.463
but who have yet to
find themselves at home.

00:13:35.370 --> 00:13:37.320
We hold close in our hearts

00:13:37.320 --> 00:13:39.450
those who are currently deployed

00:13:39.450 --> 00:13:43.160
and ask that your gentle
hand watch over them

00:13:43.160 --> 00:13:46.393
and guide them safely
back to their loved ones.

00:13:47.510 --> 00:13:51.410
May we never forget all that
our many veterans have done

00:13:51.410 --> 00:13:54.770
to protect freedom and human rights.

00:13:54.770 --> 00:13:58.310
As we work to realize
the kind of world You,

00:13:58.310 --> 00:14:01.210
our loving God intends for us,

00:14:01.210 --> 00:14:03.293
may we continue to seek peace.

00:14:04.180 --> 00:14:07.290
Peace born from wisdom and understanding,

00:14:07.290 --> 00:14:09.960
nurtured with compassion and mercy,

00:14:09.960 --> 00:14:12.850
strengthened by courage and commitment.

00:14:12.850 --> 00:14:15.333
For this we say, Amen.

00:14:16.266 --> 00:14:19.099
(patriotic music)

00:14:27.387 --> 00:14:30.054
(flag flapping)

